# Shell scripts, container configuration, and strict text formats are read by
# Linux shells, containers, and CI runners. A checkout with core.autocrlf=true
# would hand them a carriage return at the end of the shebang or key lines, and
# every invocation would fail with an error that names an invisible character.
# The development machine checks out with autocrlf enabled, so without these
# rules the next checkout would quietly produce scripts and images that cannot
# start.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# The operating tools of scripts/ops are Python and are started by their shebang
# on a Linux box, which puts them under the same rule as the shell scripts above:
# a carriage return at the end of "#!/usr/bin/env python3" makes the kernel look
# for an interpreter whose name ends in an invisible character. Python itself
# does not care about the line ending, the exec does, and that is the whole
# distinction. Narrow to this directory on purpose: the Python of backend/ is
# imported and never executed by its shebang, and a rule over every .py in the
# repository would renormalise files this reason does not cover.
scripts/ops/*.py text eol=lf

# The Python measurement scripts of docs/measurements are started on the load
# test box by their own shebang, exactly like the operating tools above, which
# puts them under the same rule for the same reason. The comment of the block
# above narrows its rule to scripts/ops with the argument that the Python of
# backend/ is imported and never executed by its shebang. That argument does
# not hold for these files: they are copied onto a Linux box and run there as
# ./<name>.py. The rule prevents the next case, and the run plan of a
# measurement additionally calls every script as python3 <file>, which
# survives the current one. Both are wanted and not one of the two, because
# five files were already in the tree with a carriage return behind the
# shebang before this rule existed.
docs/measurements/**/skripte/*.py text eol=lf

# The reference corpus is byte exact test data. Its checksums are the evidence
# of the read only invariant, so no checkout may ever rewrite a byte in it. Two
# of the files would not merely change their hash: a carriage return inside a
# PDF shifts every offset in the cross reference table and turns a valid
# document into a broken one. Git's own binary heuristic does not protect them,
# because these PDFs contain no NUL byte and would be treated as text.
testdata/corpus/** -text

# The ground truth of the OCR quality measurement. The generator writes it with a
# plain newline and compares it byte for byte on the next run, so a checkout that
# turned those newlines into carriage return pairs would make build_corpus.py
# --check fail on a machine where nothing is wrong.
testdata/corpus-truth.json text eol=lf

# The typeface the corpus is rendered with, and the licence text that has to
# travel with it. The font decides what OCR reads, so its checksum is pinned in
# scripts/dev/build_corpus.py and a checkout that rewrote a byte of it would
# fail the build rather than change the pixels quietly.
testdata/fonts/** -text
